\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Oct Dollar Inflows Raise Talk Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 20:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Adds more background, details) \par  \par    By Gerald Jeffris \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)--Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to pour into Brazil in October, further stoking concerns that the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to gain ground against the dollar, and raising speculation about more government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 A net $6.92 billion flowed into the country in October, well above the average $1.9 billion seen in the first nine months of the year, as investors dumped the damaged dollar and sought out Brazil's real, lured by strong economic growth and high local interest rates. A total of $24 billion has flowed in so far this year, up from $22.86 billion in a similar period in 2009.\par \par \uc2 "The supply of dollars locally remains much greater than demand, so pressure for strengthening of the real is likely to continue unless the government takes further actions," said Mario Paiva, trader at the BGC Liquidez brokerage in Rio de Janeiro. More measures could be forthcoming if the real approaches BRL1.65 per dollar, he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  also reported that the banks in Brazil increased their bets against the dollar in the futures market to $12.85 billion in October, from $12.43 billion in September. Higher short dollar positions usually mean investors expect the dollar to weaken down the line.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the real ended trading on the local BM&FBovespa exchange at BRL1.6773 per dollar, nearly 1.4% stronger on the day. The real has gained about 3% against the dollar since the beginning of the year, and is 12% stronger than its weakest point this year, in May.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the government raised the financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  tax, known as the IOF, on foreign investments in some local financial assets to 6% from 2%, in an effort to slow heavy foreign portfolio investment. Other measures that the government is believed to be considering include the use of reverse swap options, which would reduce pressure on the real in the futures market, and additional tax increases.\par \par \uc2 Investment into Brazil totaled $5.14 billion in October, well above an average of $2.39 billion seen over the first nine months of this year, while trade inflows totaled $1.78 billion.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega said the steps the government has already taken had prevented the strong inflows from taking too much effect on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but said Wednesday's decision by the U.S. Federal Reserve to inject another $600 billion into the U.S. economy through bond purchases would have a detrimental effect for Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "We're not seeing a strengthening of the real, because of measures that we have taken," said Mantega. "But it's a problem that could be aggravated as long as the U.S. persists in its policies."\par \par \uc2 Mantega is scheduled to travel with Brazilian President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva to the meeting of the Group of 20 heads of state in Seoul next week to discuss a resolution to what the minister has called global "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars."\par \par \uc2 The appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has caused concerns among Brazilian industry leaders and exporters, who argue the strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is encouraging imports and undermining the competitiveness of Brazilian goods abroad.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders such as Paiva, meanwhile, point out that investors will likely remain attracted to Brazilian markets as long as the country maintains elevated interest rates. Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set the country's reference Selic interest rate at 10.75% as part of its policy to combat accelerating inflation.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. Treasury continues to issue dollars and banks take the money and come here seeking higher returns," he said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported it bought $7.6 billion on the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in October, pushing the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves up to a record $285.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 -By Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; (5561) 3335-0832, gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com [ 04-11-10 1955GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101104e6b4001r8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
