\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Dollar Index Targets New Downtrend Lows\b0\par\par\uc2 1,396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 13:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1245 GMT [Dow Jones] The Dollar Index, at 77.058, is liable to dip this week to a new low for the five-month-old dowtrend which would be 75.930. The current downtrend low is 76.144, and so 76.144 is a logical profit-taking level. Daily resistance is 77.758.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar, at Y80.38, dipped earlier to a new low for the its downtrend against the yen since 1995, which is Y80.22. It's going for 79.75 support. Daily resistance is Y81.53.\par \par \uc2 The euro, at $1.3926, is vulnerable to dip to daily support at $1.3837. Resistance is $1.3961.\par \par \uc2 (stephen.cox@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1243 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazil's new government under President-elect Dilma Rousseff is unlikely to change current restrictive monetary policies, according to Banco Schahin chief economist Silvio Campos Neto. "It is probable the new president will keep to existing policies at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Campos Neto, "there is a need for adjustment on the fiscal side," he adds, because of continued heavy government spending, "but it is too early to say what's going to happen in that area." Rousseff was elected Brazil's first woman president Sunday and will take office Jan. 1; she has pledged broad continuity with existing, generally orthodox economic policies. (Rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1242 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors don't need another sign that the Fed will initiate further easing on Wednesday, but today's consumer report gave one, anyway. The core price index for personal consumption expenditures, which excludes food and energy, was flat in September compared to August. Year over year, the gauge is up just 1.2%. That's the lowest level since September 2001 and below the Fed's goal of around 2.0%. Fed officials have expressed concern about the low level of inflation, which is forcing companies to keep prices low in order to lure buyers. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1240 GMT [Dow Jones] While corporations are raking in cash, the same can't be said for households. Personal income fell by 0.1% in September after a 0.4% rise in August. It was the first decline in income since July 2009. Thinner paychecks held back spending gains, with spending up only 0.2% in September after increasing by 0.5% the previous two months. The lack of cash raises questions about how healthy the important holiday shoping season will be. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] Longer-term Treasurys higher Monday as busy week kicks off. Data early Mon. showed U.S. consumer spent only modestly ahead of key holiday season despite very low prices. All eyes on the Fed this week as investors look to policymakers to end QE debate, revealing some details about size and scope of another large scale bond buying program. Before Fed on Wednesday, market has elections to get through and will receive several pieces of key data. ISM mfg index at 10 a.m. ET. Treasury will announce a 10-yr TIPS reopening later in the day. 10-yr up 11/32 to 2.563%, 30-yr up 24/32 to 3.9393%, 2-yr flat to 0.344%. (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] CAD is little moved, hovering near late Fri's levels as a blockbuster global economic week gets under way. USD is at 1.0192, from 1.0199 late Fri, according to CQG. CAD likely will take cues this week week from broader USD moves. "Canadian markets will largely sit back with a bowl of popcorn and enjoy the show for much of this week," said Doug Porter at BMO Capital Markets in Toronto. (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1235 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD inches back to 1.3923 after US consumer spending data disappoints. Pair down from 1.3947 late Fri, and has shed overnight gains made after strong Chinese manufacturing data and draw toward riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1231 GMT [dow Jones] Consumer spending rose 0.2% in Sept after increasing by 0.5% the previous two months. Income, however, fell by 0.1% in Sept after a 0.4% rise in August. It was the first decline in income since July 2009. Economists expected spending would climb by 0.4% and income by 0.2%. The national savings rate fell to 5.3% from 5.6% in August. (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1226 GMT [Dow Jones] EUROPEAN SUMMARY: The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market seems to batten down the hatches in Europe after a whippy session in Tokyo while fixed income markets rally and equities make small gains. The major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs keep tight ranges after being spooked by USD/JPY's one big figure rally in Tokyo from a fresh 15-year low of 80.21. At first thought to be related to an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the move looks instead to be linked to an electronic trading error and has quickly reversed. Europe came in to find USD/JPY around 80.45 from its 81.55 spike high and it has traded 10 ticks either side of the former through the session. EUR/USD recovered from its spike low of 1.3894 and trades between 1.3993-1.3934 with no data to influence trade. Sterling rallies on an above-forecast 54.9 print of October manufacturing PMI against 53.0 expected with GBP/USD adding half a cent to 1.6089, although those gains have been trimmed. Fixed income markets rally as yield spreads on peripheral euro-zone government bonds start widening again. Ireland is the worst performer, with the 10-year Irish/German 10-year government bond yield spread widening 17bps to 457.2bps. December bunds trade 0.64 firmer at 129.87 while December gilts have added 0.54 to 123.90. The main European bourses are between flat and 0.3% higher, gold is up just $1.50 at $1361.25oz and oil trades $0.70c higher at $82.14. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1216 GMT [Dow Jones] Corporate bond action should lighten up this week until market digests Fed news, after heavy volume last week. The average daily trade volume in high- grade corporates was up 3.3% last week from the prior week, totaling $12.4B. Still, trade count was down 2.3%. BAC's 5.625% 2020 was the most active issue with estimated volume of $829M, ending the week at a spread of 246 bps, -13 bps on the week. GNW's 8.625% due 2016 was the worst performer, ending at 472 bps, +44 bps on the week, according to MarketAxess. (kellie.geressy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1211 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to see more government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market once President-elect Dilma Rousseff takes office in Janaury, says Carlos Gandolfo, partner in Sao Paulo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  brokerage Pioneer. "What we've seen of Rousseff so far indicates she will be more likely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to depreciate the Brazilian real against the dollar," says Gandolfo. Current President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva has taken actions such as tax hikes on foreign inflows in order to depreciate the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with the aim of boosting exports. "We could see more aggressive measures along these lines next year," says Gandolfo. (tom.murphy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1209 GMT [Dow Jones] USD fell to fresh 15-year-low against JPY early Monday before investors back off, reluctant to test possibility of more Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Pair hit Y80.21, closest yet to post-WWII low. Robust Chinese manufacturing data contribute to rise in riskier assets--pushing EUR past $1.40 overnight--before USD gains poise and reverses losses. This is a week filled with events that will likely influence \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for months, with Fed decision on QE Wed the main driver. Personal income and outlays and ISM manufacturing data due at 8:30am ET and 10am ET, respectively. EUR/USD at 1.3931 from 1.3947 late Fri, according to EBS via CQG. USD/JPY at 80.42 from 80.40. GBP/USD at 1.6062 from 1.6039.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-01-10 0845ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101101e6b10007v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
