\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Could See More  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  In 2011\b0\par\par\uc2 1,285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 13:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1211 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to see more government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market once President-elect Dilma Rousseff takes office in Janaury, says Carlos Gandolfo, partner in Sao Paulo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  brokerage Pioneer. "What we've seen of Rousseff so far indicates she will be more likely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to depreciate the Brazilian real against the dollar," says Gandolfo. Current President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva has taken actions such as tax hikes on foreign inflows in order to depreciate the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with the aim of boosting exports. "We could see more aggressive measures along these lines next year," says Gandolfo. (tom.murphy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1209 GMT [Dow Jones] USD fell to fresh 15-year-low against JPY early Monday before investors back off, reluctant to test possibility of more Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Pair hit Y80.21, closest yet to post-WWII low. Robust Chinese manufacturing data contribute to rise in riskier assets--pushing EUR past $1.40 overnight--before USD gains poise and reverses losses. This is a week filled with events that will likely influence \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for months, with Fed decision on QE Wed the main driver. Personal income and outlays and ISM manufacturing data due at 8:30am ET and 10am ET, respectively. EUR/USD at 1.3931 from 1.3947 late Fri, according to EBS via CQG. USD/JPY at 80.42 from 80.40. GBP/USD at 1.6062 from 1.6039.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1152 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades little changed in a very slow market at 24.583-24.611 24.600. With all neighboring markets closed, the EUR/CZK trades are likely to remain calm Monday, a local trader says. Some activity may pick up Tuesday and Wednesday as investors get ready for the Thursday's rate-setting meeting, the trader adds. (leos.rousek@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1145 GMT [Dow Jones] The biggest worry regarding Brazil among sovereign debt investors is "the increase in current expenditures" under incumbent President Lula, "a trend unlikely to reverse" under successor Dilma Rousseff, according to Standard Chartered Bank in a research note. Rousseff was elected Brazilian president Sunday night and will take office January 1; Standard foresees little impact from the election on Brazilian equities or the Brazilian real since a Rousseff win was widely priced in. (tom.murphy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1134 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spread rises 5bps since their open on Monday to stand at 820bps. Turnover is low at EUR15M on the local HDAT and European bond trading platforms. "Had it not been that last week we saw a dramatic spread widening, the deputy PM's comments suggesting a debt restructuring is not off the table for Greece would have been devastating," says a senior local trader. "The trend for this week is clearly for a further widening move since to date we have not seen any technical resistance levels holding," he adds. (nick.skrekas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1112 GMT [Dow Jones] While EUR/USD holds above key support at 1.3888 near-term risks are on the upside and toward the recent highs of 1.4159 and on a break here, 1.4580 says MIG Bank's Howard Friend. On the downside a break of 1.3888 will put pressure on the 1.3698 recent low and below here signals a top has been seen and opens risk toward 1.35 and 1.3334 says Friend. EUR/USD now at 1.3962. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1052 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro Overnight Index Average fixed lower Friday at 0.724% versus Thursday's 0.785%. Monday, the interbank overnight rate is trading lower between 0.60% and 0.73%, from between 0.65% and 0.75% Friday. A money dealer notes trading is very subdued due to a public holiday in some German states. Adds the liquidity level is comfortable after the ECB's 3-month tender on Thursday. Earlier Monday, 3-month Euribor fixed at 1.046% vs 1.045% Friday. (joerg.jaeger@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1044 GMT [Dow Jones] Flight-to-quality trading is hitting Irish bonds particularly hard Monday as the 10-year Irish/German yield spread tests new highs at 460 bps, 19 bps wider on the day. The weakness of the Irish financial sector is the main reason why Irish bonds are the worst-performing peripheral bond, says a London-based trader, with Portugal finding support after the government and the main opposition agreed on the 2011 budget ahead of the November 3 parliamentary vote. The 10-year Portuguese/German spread is 4 bps wider at 345 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1043 GMT [Dow Jones] Safe haven Treasurys are up in London trading as yield spreads on peripheral euro zone government bonds widen, with European equities also paring gains. The market is expected to continue trading in a range ahead of the crucial FOMC meeting that concludes on Wednesday, with the Fed widely expected to announce another round of asset purchases. On Monday, personal income data is out at 1230 GMT, while the manufacturing ISM report is due at 1400 GMT. The 2-year note is littl