\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Brazil\b0\par\par\uc2 By Raymond Colitt    \par \uc2 817 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2010\par 21:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Oct 20 (Reuters) - An unexpectedly close battle in Brazil's presidential Oct. 31 runoff election has heightened investor unease and threatens to fuel financial market volatility in Latin America's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The ruling Workers' Party candidate Dilma Rousseff, a pragmatic leftist, nearly won an outright majority in an Oct. 3 first-round ballot and is still the favorite to become the country's next president.\par \par \uc2 But markets have begun to price in the possibility that centrist Brazilian Social Democracy Party candidate Jose Serra could pull off a surprise victory win, spurred by some polls showing Rousseff's lead narrowing.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff, a former chief of staff to President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva, has benefited from a booming economy and the president's popularity, but she has watched her lead over Serra narrow substantially ahead of the runoff..\par \par \uc2 With some opinion polls suggesting Serra, a former Sao Paulo state governor favored by some business leaders for his managerial experience, was within striking distance of an upset, bonds rallied and interest rate futures dipped on expectations he would cut government expenditures.\par \par \uc2 Expansive government spending, growing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in natural resource industries, such as oil and minerals, and volatile political support for the next president are among the risks to watch in Brazil over the next year.\par \par \uc2 ECONOMIC POLICY\par \par \uc2 Both candidates are expected to keep most of the market-friendly policies of Lula's center-left government.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff, however, is likely to continue heightening the role of state enterprises -- as Lula has done -- in strategic industries such as energy, oil and telecommunications, and steer clear of sweeping labor or fiscal reforms that some investors say would improve \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's business climate.\par \par \uc2 Serra has suggested a more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  role in monetary and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and could scrap plans to increase state control over massive new oil reserves. He has called for lower interest rates, says the real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overvalued and wants the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to follow the government's economic policy.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff has praised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said it should keep focusing on controlling inflation before considering economic and job growth when setting monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 There are doubts about how firmly the candidates would push for a second generation of structural reforms to ensure Brazil's international competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 Both agree on the need to overhaul complicated tax system to encourage investment, but they have provided few details. Serra wants to reform the pension system by cutting benefits for some civil servants, while Rousseff plays down the need for far-reaching reforms soon.\par \par \uc2 Neither proposes nationalizing private companies, but Rousseff's proposal to strengthen state firms could weaken private sector participation in banking, oil and utilities.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff has said the growth of state banks would not infringe on private banks, which she said were necessary to drive economic expansion and spur competition.\par \par \uc2 Plans to use state-owned telephone company Telebras to expand broadband Internet access are moving ahead faster than expected, which could affect the share performance of private telecoms competing for the same market.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff also has adopted Lula's proposal to strengthen state-owned power company Eletrobras, though the intent may be to expand mostly abroad.\par \par \uc2 Serra is widely believed to be tougher than Rousseff on fiscal policy, even though he has made a series of electoral proposals, including a rise in the minimum wage and pension benefits, which would increase government spending by an estimated 1 percent of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Cutting administrative expenditures, as he did in the Sao Paulo state and city governments, could offset such cost increases, but public accounts would likely improve more gradually and interest rates fall more slowly than some investors had initially assumed in a Serra administration.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff has pledged an ambitious primary budget surplus target of 3.3 percent of GDP but, wary of risking her poll lead, has been noncommittal on unpopular budget cuts.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Serra rising further in the polls\par \par \uc2 -- Details of Rousseff proposals for state enterprises.\par \par \uc2 -- Signs that Rousseff is preparing austerity measures to meet her budget target early in office.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT SPENDING\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government is maintaining a high level of spending before the election, potentially fueling inflation and forcing the incoming president to adopt austerity measures.\par \par \uc2 Public spending rose sharply in 2009, cutting the primary budget surplus to an eight-year low of 2 percent of GDP. Finance Minister Guido Mantega has vowed to raise the surplus to 3.3 percent of GDP in 2010 -- in the 12 months to August it was 2.01 percent.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Continued weak monthly primary surplus figures would indicate worsening fiscal discipline and could push up interest rate futures.\par \par \uc2 -- Signs that Lula could tighten spending after the election to spare Rousseff from implementing unpopular austerity measures.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ELECTION/RISK (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101020e6ak001g5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
