\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil's real sinks on tax rise, China move\b0\par\par\uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 16:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * China's surprise interest rate hike boosts U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real down 1.5 pct, Mexican peso loses 0.7 pct\par \par \uc2 * Analysts doubt long-term success of Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures\par \par \uc2 * Chile's peso sinks 1.1 pct as copper prices fall (Adds comments by Brazil's finance minister, context, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Oct 19 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real dropped sharply on Tuesday after an interest rate hike in China lent Brazil's government a hand in its fight to curb a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian government late Monday raised the tax it charges foreigners to buy local bonds and also increased the levy on any derivatives trading they do, hoping to scare away foreign investors the government blames for driving up the real.\par \par \uc2 The real <BRBY> was headed for its biggest one-day loss in about four months on Tuesday, sinking 1.48 percent to 1.689 reais per U.S. dollar on the local spot market.\par \par \uc2 But with gains of about 100 percent since 2003, the real is still the world's most overvalued major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp losses against the dollar on Tuesday had just as much to do with China's decision to raise interest rates, adding that the increase to Brazil's so-called IOF tax would not keep foreigners away for long. For details, see [ID:nN18280200]\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is reflecting what's going on abroad just as much as the IOF," said Luciano Rostagno, chief strategist for CM Capital Markets in Sao Paulo. "China's interest rate hike is making the dollar strengthen, which is also causing the real to depreciate."\par \par \uc2 The dollar's sharp gains caused weakness across the range of Latin America's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=> dropped 0.74 percent to 12.5016 per dollar as investors saw China's announcement as an excuse to take profits following gains of roughly 6 percent since the beginning of September.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso <CLP=> lost 1.1 percent to 486.10 per dollar as the strong greenback also pushed down the price of copper, the country's main export. A Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told reporters on Tuesday that the bank was not currently discussing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nDEP003370]\par \par \uc2 CALL FOR HELP\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister, Guido Mantega, later Tuesday praised China's decision to raise interest rates, saying the hike was "in the right direction." [ID:nN19130365]\par \par \uc2 In the lead-up to this week's G20 finance ministers meeting in South Korea, Mantega has urged countries such as the United States to help ease the problems of emerging economies, saying Brazil could not fight the global "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" alone.\par \par \uc2 A weak U.S. dollar and low interest rates across much of the developed world have attracted a flood of money into emerging economies, forcing countries from Colombia to Thailand to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 A strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes exports more expensive and can cause long-term damage to a country's economy.\par \par \uc2 Speculation has mounted that the U.S. Fed will decide soon to pump more money into its struggling economy. Such a measure could keep the dollar weak and yields on U.S. Treasuries close to record lows, encouraging even more foreign investors to seek higher-yielding bonds in countries such as Brazil.\par \par \uc2 After hiking the IOF tax on bonds only two weeks earlier, Brazil's government raised its tax on foreign bond purchases to 6 percent from 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 The government also announced on Monday it will increase the tax on the collateral that foreign investors have to put up when they want to trade Brazilian derivatives -- to 6 percent from 0.38 percent.\par \par \uc2 But analysts doubted the effectiveness of the moves. The tax on bond purchases would need to be much higher to push investors away from Brazil's high yields and attractive economy, they said. Also, the government needs a constant stream of so-called "hot money" to plug it's growing current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 "Fundamentals are solid for Brazil and we will be ready to resume recommending buying the real as soon as the dust settles," analysts at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  said in a note to clients Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The reaction in the bond and interest rate futures market was limited. The yield on the 10-year fixed-rate, real-denominated note, known as NTN-F <BR10YT=RR>, rose slightly as investors moved out of the market.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the contract due January 2012 <DIJF2>, one of the most heavily traded contracts early in the session, rose to 11.29 percent from 11.28 percent. (Additional reporting by Caroline Stauffer in Mexico City, editing by Todd Benson and Jeffrey Benkoe)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|U|RBN|D|M|RNP|DNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101022e6aj0019d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
