\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Mexico Likely To Stay Neutral In  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Battles\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Guthrie  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \uc2 882 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 17:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Latin American countries fighting what some governments see as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war aren't likely to find an ally in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Amid global tensions over exchange rates, Brazil has raised a tax on certain financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to discourage the heavy foreign capital inflows that are boosting its real, while Thailand this week removed a tax break on foreign investment in its government bonds in an attempt to slow the baht's rise, and the U.S. Federal Reserve appears poised for another round of quantitative easing that could weaken the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso still has room to recover from the beating it took in 2008 and 2009, despite its own recent appreciation. The peso is trading around MXN12.40 to the dollar, up 6.5% year-to-date and 26% from its all-time low in March 2009, but still nearly 20% weaker from before the 2008 crisis. Brazil's real, meanwhile, is up 5% year-to-date but 41% stronger from the end of 2008.\par \par \uc2 Mexican policy makers appear loathe to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the peso and give Mexican exports a competitive advantage.\par \par \uc2 During a weekend visit to Washington, Finance Minister Ernesto Cordero said his country "believes and trusts" in a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has allowed Mexico to absorb external shocks in the past. Cordero said countries need to be careful when implementing "easy fixes" such as capital controls, noting that the Brazilian real continues to appreciate despite such measures. Exchange policy shouldn't be a trade policy tool, he added.\par \par \uc2 Cordero described Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  circumstance as "fortunately different" than that of some other Latin American countries, alluding to recent export gains in the U.S. market. Mexican products now represent 13% of U.S. imports compared with 10% just three years ago, Cordero said, attributing the advance to structural advantages that Mexico boasts over other countries--its geographical location, border trade infrastructure, and improving labor productivity--rather than relying on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  advantages.\par \par \uc2 "It's evident that Mexico is a stand-alone case compared with South American countries because of its greater exposure to the U.S. trade balance. We have seen that when the risk premium rises due to uncertainty about the U.S. recovery, the peso is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that most suffers," said Moises Junca, chief Latin America \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with BBVA Securities.\par \par \uc2 Yet some see Mexico's export gains as a crutch.\par \par \uc2 "The Mexican peso has been a laggard among the region's appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a reason: whereas other countries can rely on some degree of domestic demand strength to cushion their economies against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, the Mexican recovery remains externally driven," Luis Arcentales, an economist with \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , wrote in a Monday research note. "And with export-oriented industry already decelerating, Mexico seems to be the economy that can least afford significant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength."\par \par \uc2 Gross domestic product expanded 5.9% in the first half of the year and is expected to slow in the second half for full-year growth between 4% and 5%. Below-capacity output and low inflation have allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold its benchmark overnight lending rate at 4.5% since July 2009, with no signs that it's contemplating a rate increase before the second half of 2011. Brazil's benchmark selic rate is 10.75% and expected to rise early next year.\par \par \uc2 The rebound in exports after a trade slump in 2009, rising crude prices, and remittances from Mexicans living in the U.S. are all sources of peso strength. Also this year, Mexico's Oct. 1 inclusion in Citibank's World Government Bond Index is seen attracting as much as $16 billion in fresh foreign investment, much of which has presumably already arrived.\par \par \uc2 While policy makers in countries like \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Colombia and Peru are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, Mexico has adhered to a comparatively subtle mechanism of selling $600 million in dollar put options each month. The Bank of Mexico uses the options to build foreign reserves when the peso is appreciating; commercial banks can exercise the options when the peso is stronger than its 20-day average.\par \par \uc2 Recent peso gains have led to some discussion on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might opt to raise the dollar amount of the options it sells each month. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  thinks that's a possibility, but adds that "the MXN is one of the least vulnerable emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 (Amy Guthrie first began covering emerging markets in 1999. Currently she writes about Mexican financial markets from Mexico City, and is a former managing editor of the LatinFinance magazine. She can be reached at amy.guthrie@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. )\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 14, 2010 11:04 ET (15:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101014e6ae003ps\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
