\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Brazil\b0\par\par\uc2 By Raymond Colitt   \par \uc2 1,438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 06:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Oct 4 (Reuters) - Ruling party candidate Dilma Rousseff is favored to win Brazil's presidential election in a Oct. 31 runoff against opposition candidate Jose Serra.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff would likely keep most economic policies in place from President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva's center-left government, but may strengthen the state's role in specific industries.\par \par \uc2 Heavy government spending, policies to develop vast oil reserves and the new balance of power in Congress after the presidential election are among the risks to watch in Brazil over the next year.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION\par \par \uc2 Rousseff, a pragmatic leftist from the ruling Workers' Party, is expected to win the runoff after falling just short of an outright victory in the first round on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Her opponent is former Sao Paulo state governor Jose Serra, of the centrist Brazilian Social Democracy Party.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff is Lula's former chief of staff and has benefited from a booming economy and the president's huge popularity.\par \par \uc2 Unlike previous presidential races in Brazil, there is no clear market favorite as both contenders are expected to maintain most market-friendly policies that have been in place for the past decade.\par \par \uc2 Still, Rousseff and Serra both favor strong government and could extend the state's role in some parts of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Eager to win over centrist voters and avoid unsettling investors, Rousseff has emphasized policy continuity.\par \par \uc2 But she would likely heighten the role of state enterprises in strategic industries such as energy and telecommunications, a move that might pose risks for equities investors. She will probably also steer clear of sweeping reforms to labor laws or fiscal policy that some investors say would improve \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's business climate.\par \par \uc2 Serra, meanwhile, has suggested a more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  role in monetary and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, raising concern in financial markets. He has called for lower interest rates, says the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overvalued and wants the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to follow the government's economic policy.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff, a former Marxist guerrilla, has praised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said the bank should keep focusing on controlling inflation in coming years before considering economic and job growth when setting monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 There is also some doubt about how firmly the candidates would push for a second generation of structural reforms to ensure Brazil's international competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 Both agree on the need to overhaul Brazil's complicated tax system to encourage investment but they have provided few details. Serra wants to reform the pension system by cutting benefits for some civil servants, while Rousseff plays down the need for far-reaching reforms any time soon.\par \par \uc2 Neither proposes nationalizing private companies but Rousseff's proposal to strengthen state firms could weaken private sector participation in banking, oil and utilities.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff has said the growth of state banks would not infringe on private banks, which she said were necessary to drive economic expansion and spur competition.\par \par \uc2 Government plans to use state-owned telephone company Telebras to expand broadband Internet access have moved its shares and could also affect share performance of private telecoms depending on their participation in deals.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff has adopted Lula's proposal to strengthen state-owned power company Eletrobras, though the intent may be to expand mostly abroad.\par \par \uc2 Serra is widely believed to be the tougher than Rousseff on fiscal discipline.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff has pledged an ambitious primary budget surplus target of 3.3 percent of GDP but, wary of risking her large poll lead, has been noncommittal on unpopular budget cuts.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -- Details of Rousseff proposals for state enterprises.\par  -- Signs Rousseff is preparing austerity measures to meet\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 her budget target early in office.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GOVERNMENT SPENDING\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government is maintaining a high level of spending before the election, potentially fueling inflation and forcing the incoming president to adopt austerity measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Public spending rose sharply in 2009, eroding the primary budget surplus to an eight-year low of 2 percent of gross domestic product. Finance Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guido Mantega\f28 \uc2 \f2  has pledged to pursue a surplus of 3.3 percent of GDP in 2010 but in the 12 months to August it was only 2.01 percent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What to watch:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Continued weak monthly primary surplus figures would indicate worsening fiscal discipline and could push up interest rate futures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Signs that Lula could tighten spending after the election to spare Rousseff from implementing unpopular austerity measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CORRUPTION\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the next month of campaigning, more mud-slinging and corruption scandals may surface. They can not only influence swing voters but can paralyze Congress and spiral into wider scandals engulfing parties and coalitions. Lula himself came close to facing impeachment proceedings in 2005 when his party was involved in an illegal campaign-financing scandal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OIL AND GAS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's government has increased its share in state-owned oil company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2  through a massive capitalization that drew more investor appetite than expected. But there are some risks associated with the deal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2  may use part of the $70 billion it raised to invest in projects such as refineries that further economic development but have a lower return, thereby harming minority shareholder interests. Also, the government boosted its stake in the company to 48 percent of total outstanding shares, up from 40 percent previously.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A legislative framework that has been held up in the Senate but is expected to be approved after the election would further heighten state control over some of the world's biggest recent oil finds. The bill would also ensure their proceeds help bankroll state investments in areas like infrastructure, education and poverty-reduction programs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If approved, the measures will likely reduce competition in the sector while boosting the role of state energy giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2 , offering fewer but still attractive opportunities for foreign investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Critics say the laws threaten the efficiency of Brazil's successful oil sector by stifling investment and increasing the dangers of political interference and corruption.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What to watch:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Signs that Congress can agree on how to distribute oil revenue between the federal government and states, paving the way for approval of the oil bills after the election.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MINING\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government plans to send to Congress this year framework legislation designed to stimulate exploration and production. It would improve regulatory oversight and reduce the life span of mining concessions in a bid to discourage speculation in mineral properties.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industry leaders have warned that excessive restrictions could act as a disincentive to fresh investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mining authorities say that once the new legislation is in place, the government could discuss a possible hike in royalties for the mining sector, including iron ore. This could hit the bottom line of Vale, the world's largest iron ore exporter, as well as other mining and steel companies. The Rousseff camp has denied she has any plans to hike royalties.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What to watch:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Presentation of the new mining bill to Congress with possible changes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Leaks on discussions over royalties.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CONGRESSIONAL SUPPORT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rousseff's coalition won a clear majority in the Senate, which will make it easier to advance her legislative agenda if she wins the runoff. The ruling coalition also appears to have made important gains in the lower house, according to partial results from Sunday's election. But progress in Congress would depend on how well the inexperienced Rousseff can deal with often unruly allies, a challenge that even stumped the savvy Lula at times.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Serra were to win the Oct. 31 runoff, he might struggle to build a clear majority in Congress, even though legislators typically "migrate" to the governing coalition after elections. The process could create a hung parliament with little chance of approving major bills before Lula leaves office on Jan. 1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FOREIGN POLICY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If elected, Serra is likely to cool ties with some of Lula's left-wing allies in Latin America. That could affect energy investments in Bolivia and Venezuela, where Lula had prodded \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2  to invest to foster regional integration.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Serra accused the Bolivian government of turning a blind eye to cross-border drug trafficking. He has also criticized Venezuela for allegedly harboring Colombian rebels. Some analysts think Serra could take a harder line in trade disputes with Argentina and the South American trade block Mercosur.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rousseff, by contrast, has pledged to continue current foreign policy and could maintain current Foreign Minister Celso Amorim in office or name his deputy, Antonio Patriota.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But given her priority on domestic issues and her relative lower international profile, she is unlikely to be as influential in diplomacy as Lula. (Editing by Stuart Grudgings and Kieran Murray)\f28 \par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-RISKS/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|Table|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|ABX|BNX|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101004e6a40006p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
