\par\par \b \uc2 Chile's Stocks Close Higher, Following Gains In U.S. Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 967 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 22:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended higher Friday, with retailers leading the way, as it tracked rising U.S. markets.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa ended 0.2% higher at 4802.93, while market volume slipped to 82.4 billion Chilean pesos ($171.2 million) compared with CLP112.4 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 As several of the Ipsa's heavier-weighted shares also trade in New York, the Ipsa often tracks the Dow Jones Industrial Average. The DJIA grew 0.4% to 10830 although a key manufacturing report yielded conflicting signals.\par \par \uc2 Among dually listed shares, flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN), which is in the process of merging with Brazilian airline TAM (TAM, TAMM4.BR), gained 0.5% to CLP14,360.00, while the nation's largest power generator \uc2 Endesa\uc2  (EOC, ENDESA.SN) increased 0.9% to CLP882.85.\par \par \uc2 Also, Chile's second biggest bank in terms of loans, \uc2 Banco de Chile\uc2  (BCH, CHILE.SN) expanded 1.5% to CLP72.59 after it posted a 10.9% year-on-year jump in its loans business in August, slightly above growth seen in July.\par \par \uc2 Retailers, which have consistently been among the Ipsa's biggest winners as Chile quickly rebounds from last year's recession and February's devastating earthquake on strong domestic demand, again led gains.\par \par \uc2 Retail holding giant \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CENCOSUD.SN) grew 1.7% to CLP3,320.00 after local investment bank Celfin Capital increased its end-2011 target price to CLP3,800 per share and gave it a buy recommendation. Department store Falabella (FALABELLA.SN) also rose as Celfin Capital upped its year-end 2011 target price to CLP5,622 per share and gave it a buy recommendation. Falabella ended 1.1% higher at CLP4,790.00.\par \par \uc2 Despite the Ipsa's rise, specialty chemical and fertilizer producer SQM (SQM) fell as the company's more liquid B-series shares (SQM-B.SN) continued to slip following \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's recent downgrade of its stock from neutral to underperform.\par \par \uc2 "There are still a lot of investors, especially foreign investors, selling SQM after the \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  downgrade. However, the company's medium and long term perspectives look very auspicious," said analyst Raul Barros, with local brokerage BBVA Research.\par \par \uc2 Also slipping, information technology company Sonda (SONDA.SN) lost 3.2% to CLP1,054.90 as investors booked profits after its shares rose over 22% in September.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended at a 28-month high against the dollar, as international copper prices surged and the euro posted strong gains against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The peso finished at CLP481.00 to the dollar, compared to CLP483.50 the prior session, while trading in a range of CLP480.00 to CLP482.30.\par \par \uc2 With Chile producing more than one-third of the world's copper, the peso often takes cues from the metal's international prices. Copper futures in New York vaulted to two-year highs on positive economic data from China and a weaker dollar.\par \par \uc2 Also, as Europe is one of Chile's main trade partners, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does against the dollar. The euro hit a six-month peak against the dollar, after the Federal Reserve said it is almost certain to have to offer fresh support to ensure that already tepid U.S. economic growth does not continue to falter.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, local market participants digested the Brazilian real's gains to 1.6780 to the dollar despite the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strengthening. Because Brazil and Mexico are the two largest economies in Latin America, the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  often takes cues from the real's and Mexican peso's movements against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 As the strength of the Chilean peso cuts into the competitiveness of the nation's exports and because the local economy is highly dependent on those products, exporters have been clamoring for market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, isn't expected to signal that it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the peso gains to at least a range of CLP460 to CLP470 because in real terms the peso is still trading above levels that would merit an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say.\par \par \uc2 That \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the real is still strengthening against the dollar provides an argument for not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Chile.\par \par \uc2 "Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the real has continued to strengthen because there is broad dollar weakness at the international level. The way markets are reading this is that market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only provide a short term solution, but it won't be too effective over the medium or long term considering the dollar's overall weakness," said Sergio Tricio, head of studies with local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chile.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower as the peso's strength fueled expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's future interest rate hikes will be less aggressive, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates by 50 basis points for a fourth consecutive month last month as the country quickly recovers from its first recession in a decade last year and a devastating February earthquake.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said, though, that the peso's strength relieves some pressure from continuing rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.72%, from 2.73% Thursday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.99%, from 3.08% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 01-10-10 2039GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101001e6a100161\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
