\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Nations Race To Weaken  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 , Sparking Trade War Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Lauricella and John Lyons  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 1,167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 00:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Tensions are growing in the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as political rhetoric heats up and countries battle to protect their exporters, raising concerns about potentially damaging trade wars.\par \par \uc2 At least half a dozen countries are actively trying to push down the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the most high-profile of which is Japan, which is attempting to halt the rise of the yen after a 14% rise since May. In the U.S., Congress is considering a law that targets China for keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially low, and in Brazil, the head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the country to impose a tax on foreign investment, which has contributed to a big rise in the real.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal Web site, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Businesses always want to be more competitive and politicians often talk a big game. But in the current environment, as many economies are still struggling to recover from the global financial crisis, worries are growing that policy makers could be more aggressive in protecting their nation's business interests.\par \par \uc2 Rising protectionism is "a very big risk," says Erin Browne, a macro stock market strategist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . "And it's something that could move more into the spotlight after the [U.S.] election.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market strains could also be a topic of discussion at next week's IMF/World Bank meeting in Washington, where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government finance officials will be gathering.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government earlier this month stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in years. To counter the yen's rise, Japan sold some $20 billion worth of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which traders said was its biggest-ever effort in a single day.\par \par \uc2 Japan joined many other Asian emerging-market countries that have fighting rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a near daily basis, such as Taiwan, South Korea and Thailand. In Latin America, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Colombia and Peru have also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tamp down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., protectionist efforts have been on the rise, especially when it comes to China, which is widely seen as keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, at artificially low levels in order to boost its exports and make it more expensive for the Chinese to purchase goods produced abroad.\par \par \uc2 The House of Representatives is expected to pass legislation on Thursday that would let U.S. companies argue that Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy represents an unfair subsidy. Democratic Senator Charles Schumer plans to push similar legislation to punish China for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation in the lame-duck session following the election. But it is considered unlikely the bill will pass.\par \par \uc2 Even so, the administration could use the threat of congressional action to press Beijing to make further adjustments in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly as a summit of the Group-of-20 leaders in mid November draws closer.\par \par \uc2 IMF Managing Director Dominique Strauss-Kahn said he wouldn't rule out a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war and that officials at both the fund and the Group of 20 nations were actively working to prevent such a battle of competitive depreciations.\par \par \uc2 However, in a press briefing in Washington, Mr. Strauss-Kahn said he didn't believe there was a big risk of such a war.\par \par \uc2 "There is no good to expect from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. "History has shown that the effect of this kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't last for very long."\par \par \uc2 Part of the challenge is that the moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that are raising the ire of central banks and politicians are being driven by longer-term investors.\par \par \uc2 Stronger economies in the emerging markets are attracting capital from the developed world, pumping up demand for local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Talk of additional quantitative easing in the U.S. signals to investors that interest rates there will remain close to zero for a long time.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, inflation is building in Asia, forcing interest rates higher. Investors see that mismatch and are shuttling money from west to east, attracted to the higher yields.\par \par \uc2 "The flows that we're seeing are soundly based," says Richard Yetsenga, global head of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some countries \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more forcefully than others. Technology exporter Taiwan sees little fluctuation in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , thanks partly to heavy government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is up less than 2% against the dollar this year. Malaysia, on the other hand, has allowed a stronger ringgit for the independence a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives its monetary policy makers, and the spur it gives to its exporters to become more efficient.\par \par \uc2 Government officials outside China for the most part step gingerly when it comes to the region's largest economy and its approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform, if they say anything at all. Singapore Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong said last week in an interview with The Wall Street Journal regarding China's appreciation: "I can understand their caution, but on balance they need not go so slow."\par \par \uc2 The rhetoric was much hotter this week out of Brazil, where its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen more than 30% against the dollar this year as investors flock to its relatively high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Brazil's Finance Minister Guido Mantega lashed out at the U.S., Japan and other rich nations he says are letting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken to spur growth--growth that comes at the expense of other exporters like Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "We're in the midst of an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," Mr. Mantega said during an event in Sao Paulo. "This threatens us because it takes away our competitiveness."\par \par \uc2 The head of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to slow the real's rise, was more circumspect on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "There is a very serious \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  problem which should be addressed," Henrique de Campos Meirelles said. Mr. Meirelles said that raising taxes on flows into Brazil was a possibility.\par \par \uc2 Officials say Brazil's 10.75% benchmark interest rate is necessary to squelch inflation and keep the Latin America's biggest economy from overheating. But it attracts a flood of investment from speculators who borrow in the U.S. or Japan where money is cheap, and deposit it in Brazil. The inflows of cash propel the real even higher.\par \par \uc2 With Brazil's presidential elections scheduled for Sunday, dealing with a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is shaping up as the first major economic issue to face Brazil's next president. Dilma Rousseff, the former Energy Minister and handpicked successor to President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva leading the polls, is an advocate of Brazil's 11-year-old floating exchange rate. But she is likely to face pressure from economists within her left-wing party, including Mr. Mantega, to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more heavily.\par \par \uc2 (Ian Talley, Bob Davis and Alex Frangos contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 Write to Tom Lauricella at tom.lauricella@wsj.com and John Lyons at john.lyons@wsj.com [ 09-28-10 1833ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020100928e69s000tr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
