\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-World in a " \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  war," Brazil finmin says\b0\par\par\uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 15:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Strength of Brazil's real hurting exporters\par \par \uc2 * Mantega blames United States, Europe and Japan\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepping up \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson and Aluisio Alves\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The world is in an "international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" as governments manipulate their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to improve their export competitiveness, Brazilian Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Mantega's speech to Brazilian industrial leaders included some of the strongest comments to date by any senior government official on the recent bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by countries, including Japan and China.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRBY>, is now the world's most overvalued major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Near a 10-month high against the dollar, the real continued to rise after Mantega's comment as traders bet the government may be waiting for the outcome of Sunday's presidential election before taking action.\par \par \uc2 "We're in the midst of an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," Mantega said. "This threatens us because it takes away our competitiveness."\par \par \uc2 With some economies still reeling from the global financial crisis, countries have sought to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports and improve trade balances.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be a hotly disputed topic at the next meeting of the International Monetary Fund in Washington, D.C., Oct. 8-10.\par \par \uc2 Japan, Colombia, Thailand and other countries have been seeking to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help accelerate their economic recovery. For details, see [ID:nTOE68F05F].\par \par \uc2 Low interest rates across much of the developed world have also prompted investors to pour cash into higher-yielding assets in countries such as Brazil, causing further outcry.\par \par \uc2 "The advanced countries are seeking to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mantega said, mentioning the United States, Europe and Japan in the context of what he portrayed as an intensifying trade competition.\par \par \uc2 Mantega has repeatedly tried to talk down the real. The government last week threatened to use the sovereign wealth fund to buy dollars.\par \par \uc2 But the government has been reluctant to follow through with concrete measures, and traders bid up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  near the 1.70 per dollar level, which some see as the threshold for new, stronger action by the government.\par \par \uc2 Last week's share offering by state-oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  contributed to a large inflow of dollars to Brazil, which is attractive to foreign investors because of high interest rates and its booming economy.\par \par \uc2 Mantega said the country still has an arsenal of tools available to weaken the real, although he did not offer details. Mantega said the government was not considering additional taxes on foreign investments but noted that the government has imposed such controls to hold back the real in the past.\par \par \uc2 MORE MEASURES COMING?\par \par \uc2 The remarks could be a sign that Brazil's government will beef up efforts to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Raphael Martello, an analyst with Tendencias consultancy in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 "He could be preparing the way for a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, it gave other countries a justification to do the same thing," Martello said.\par \par \uc2 Tony Volpon, head of emerging market research for the Americas at Nomura Securities, added that Mantega's could also simply be "a conditional threat he hopes not to have to act upon."\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen on Sept. 15, the first such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004. [ID:nTOE68E02W]\par \par \uc2 And the United States has vowed to rally global heavyweights on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, which many economists say is undervalued. (Editing by Brian Winter and Kenneth Barry)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|Z|ABX|BNX|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100927e69r001el\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
