\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 18:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par US DURABLE GOODS ORDERS DROP 1.3% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand for U.S. manufactured durable goods tumble more than expected in August, to an adjusted $191.17 billion, held back by steep drops in airplanes and cars. It was the biggest drop since August 2009. Economists were looking for a smaller decline of 1.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US AUGUST NEW-HOME SALES FALL SHORT OF EXPECTATIONS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. new home sales come in unchanged from July at a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 288,000, below economists' expectations of 295,000, as consumers lacking a government tax incentive are cautious in the face of high unemployment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCKS RALLY FOLLOWING ECONOMIC DATA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks climb broadly, putting the market on pace for its fourth-straight weekly gain as investors are encouraged by readings on housing and capital spending by businesses that point toward a stabilizing economy. DJIA gains more than 150 points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NORWAY'S \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SUES CITIGROUP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Norway's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sues \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  over alleged misstatements about the company's financial condition, which it claims caused it to buy Citi shares at inflated prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU REGULATOR TO REVIEW ROCHE'S AVASTIN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An advisory panel of the European Medicines Agency launches a review of Roche's cancer drug Avastin for the treatment of metastatic breast cancer, raising concerns about the medicine's risk-benefit profile.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BRAZIL SEES POSSIBLE CREDIT RISK IN DOLLAR FLOWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Henrique Meirelles says heavy dollar inflows could pose credit risk for the country's banking system. That is why \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has taken \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measures in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, he says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HOUSE PANEL OKS CHINA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LEGISLATION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Legislation that targets imports from China and other countries with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  similarly perceived to be undervalued sails through U.S. House Ways and Means Committee, preparing way for vote in full chamber perhaps as soon as next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MOTOROLA PLANS VOTE ON REVERSE STOCK SPLIT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shareholders will vote on the reverse stock split on Nov. 29. The split will range between 1-for-3 and 1-for-7 and is intended to raise the company's stock price before a planned split of the company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PORTUGAL HIT BY OPPOSITION AUSTERITY BUDGET STANCE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Portugal's standing in credit markets weakens further as the country's main opposition party continues to deny support for the minority government's 2011 austerity budget, the key to shoring up Portugal's public finances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CERBERUS PLANS REVIVAL OF CHRYSLER FINANCIAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cerberus Capital Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  is plotting a revival of Chrysler Financial, the auto lender whose core business was effectively dissolved by the U.S. Treasury, sources say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par The Wall Street Journal \par WHY THE FSA CAN'T SHOOT THE MESSENGER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before we journalists mount our collective high horse about the FSA's anti-leak measures, we should ask ourselves a question. Does anybody seriously think that the authorities have a hope in hell of stopping leaks? asks Joe Ortiz.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Heard on the Street \par THREE KEY ISSUES IRELAND MUST ADDRESS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Irish 10-year bond yields have soared to 6.6%, 4.3 percentage points more than on German debt, generating fears that it may have to follow Greece in asking for a bailout. How does Ireland get out of this hole? Questions, Richard Barley.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Practice Management \par NOT LEAVING AN HEIR TOO MUCH, TOO SOON \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Preparing for the worst is never easy, but it can be even more complicated when a loved one is still a teenager. Wayne Copelin came up with a plan to not only protect the estates holdings, but the heir from herself, writes Peter McDougall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Analysis \par THE ECONOMY CAN'T GROW FOREVER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 People may not want to admit it, but there are limits to the growth developed countries have experienced over the past few decades, and the reality is we may have to learn to live new personal, and economic, lives, writes Rex Nutting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US STOCKS CLIMB BROADLY FOLLOWING HOUSING, DURABLE-GOODS DATA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 US stocks surge as fears over European economic growth ease.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TREASURYS FALL MORE AFTER BRIEF PAUSE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys fall more after a brief pause.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUR/USD HITS INTRADAY HIGH; TARGETING 1.35 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar slips further against the euro on better-than-expected euro zone data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CRUDE UP ON IMPROVING GERMAN ECONOMY, REFINERY OUTAGE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil is up early Friday on solid German data, a refinery shutdown and a tropical storm.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMEX GOLD HITS $1,300/OZ AS DOLLAR FALLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold hits $1,300 a troy ounce as the dollar weakens. [ 24-09-10 1600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100924e69o000zt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
