\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Cost Of  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Grows - BBH\b0\par\par\uc2 1,113 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2010\par 14:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1252 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the widening interest rate differential between the US and Brazil, the cost to the Brazilian government of buying dollars is becoming more expensive, says BBH. Brazil issues bonds to finance its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases; this is contributing to its growing budget deficit, which reached an eight-month high in July, BBH says. At the same time, growth differentials and strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  contribute to the yawning current account deficit, which is approaching record levels, BBH says. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1241 GMT [Dow Jones] The starts report shows housing completions in August rose 5.6% to annual rate of 603,000, with single-family housing completions up 1.3%, to 482,000. That pace is well above the recent pace of new home sales, and (while there are differences in data measurements) it suggests builders are still adding to supply. But at least progress has been made: The total completion rate is 23.7% below the August 2009 level.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1240 GMT [Dow Jones] While budget deficits have generally narrowed in peripheral euro zone countries, with the exception of Portugal, the lack of revenue growth remains a worry, says Janet Henry at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Things may only get tougher from here on as the end of the month brings strikes against some of the structural reforms that are being implemented in Spain and Portugal. "The global growth picture is likely to be less helpful in the next few quarters and, after the recent VAT increases fall out of the annual comparison, inflation is set to fall sharply again from mid-2011 implying the debt burden will not be eroded by stronger nominal growth," Henry says. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1238 GMT [Dow Jones] The CDX North American Investment Grade Index Series 15 is trading 0.3% tighter at 105.8/106.8BP after huge jump in housing starts. The close last night was 106.61BP. Stock futures also rose on the news, DJIA futures up 24. (katy.burne@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY rises back to 85.50 from 85.39 right after Aug housing starts surprises to the upside. USD/JPY continues to trade at lower end of level following last week's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese gov't. Pair at 85.75 late Mon, according to EBS via CQG. EUR/USD down slightly at 1.3114 from 1.3120 level mins before data, and up from 1.3061 late Mon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] Building permits increased 1.8% to an annual rate of 569,000 in August, compared with flat reading expected. Meanwhile, the 10.5% rise in housing starts was evident in single family, up 4.3%, and buildings of more than 2 units, which jumped 32.2% in August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1234 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasury prices remain higher despite better-than-expected housing starts as investors remain focused on the Fed. Investors are on tenterhooks about whether or not the Fed will announce another large scale bond buying program, or allude to the possibility that one could be coming. 10-yr up 7/32 to 2.679%, 30-yr up 12/32 to 3.851%, 2-yr flat to 0.456%. DJIA futures up 20. (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1234 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazil authorities "successfully spooked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets" with the announcement late Monday that the Sovereign Wealth Fund was authorized to buy dollars in the market, making it a dominant player alongside the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says RBS Securities. It was enough to push USDBRL close to key resistance 1.7350 for a potential break higher, RBS says. The government says there are "no limits" on the amount of purchases; this is a reference to the allocation of the fund into dollars as opposed to the absolute amount of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that the SWF can buy, RBS says. Locals estimate the SWF at BRL18 billion, or roughly $10 billion, RBS says. Still, the government may choose to hold back some of its firepower, including use of SWF, until after the mammoth share issuance by the state oil company, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2 , later this month, it says. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1233 GMT [Dow Jones] Housing starts unexpectedly jump in August, rising 10.5% to annual rate of 598K--much better the 0.2% dip expected. Economists had viewed August as a good reading of housing industry now that we are a few months away from the end of the tax credit for buyers. The increase suggests housing can improve even without government help.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1225 GMT [Dow Jones] There is a reason to fear a housing number that could disappoint, writes Lena Komileva of Tullett Prebon. "In the US, housing starts have collapsed by 20% since the expiration of the government's housing tax credit deadline, which revealed a much weaker trend in underlying private sector demand," she says. "This equates to about 560k in excess vacant homes in the market....This is likely to continue to exert pressure on both housing starts and home prices." EUR/USD at 1.3119, from 1.3061 late Mon. USD/JPY at 85.38 from 85.75.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1212 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasury prices are up only slightly Tues. morning as trading slows ahead of FOMC. Most don't think the Fed will announce another large scale bond buying program after its meeting, but are looking for details that allude to if, and when such a program could come. Many in the Tsy market believe another program could be unveiled before year-end, sending Tsy prices up. Before the Fed Tues., Aug housing starts, forecast to slip 0.2% at 8:30 ET. 10-yr up 4/32 to 2.688%, 30-yr up 8/32 to 3.855% and 2-yr flat to 0.460%. (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1211 GMT [Dow Jones] Santander is skeptical that the Brazilian government will step up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the Brazilian real. "Although the technical positioning seems less favorable for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems to have reached another level, we still believe that strong USD inflows and the lure of the carry and growth trades will prove formidable obstacles against BRL depreciation in the short term," it says, adding that it "still see the real testing the 1.7 mark in the short term." (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 0852ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100921e69l0008z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
