\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil not mulling inflows tax for now-govt source\b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 20:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Capital inflows tax (IOF) is a last resort-source\par \par \uc2 * Government is trying to manage expectations\par \par \uc2 * Cenbank has recently stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Ana Nicolaci da Costa\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Sept 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry will not for now use a tax on capital inflows to control the amount of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flooding the country and swelling the real, a government source told Reuters on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The comments accompany moves by the government to step up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the real <BRBY>, ranked the world's most overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , from rallying further.\par \par \uc2 "The IOF tax would be the last resort," the source said on condition of anonymity because of the sensitivity of the issue. "What would facilitate the work and maybe (would reduce) the need to use the sovereign wealth fund is if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market at both ends."\par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry declined to comment on the issue when contacted by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been calling two auctions a day rather than just one since last Wednesday to buy dollars on the spot market. There is increasing speculation it could also offer reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps -- a form of derivative which would have the same effect as buying dollars in the futures market.\par \par \uc2 Brazil is gearing up for massive inflows of U.S. dollars from an imminent stock offering for the capitalization of oil giant \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  . It is also struggling with a widening current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 This has helped push the real 4.8 percent higher since the end of June, even though it is only up 1.3 percent on the year.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  is also worried about the level of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which makes the country less competitive by reducing the price of imports and making exports more expensive. See [ID:nN15168058]\par \par \uc2 He has said the ministry would take the necessary steps to prevent an excessive appreciation, increasing speculation it could soon use Brazil's sovereign wealth fund to buy dollars and do its bit to tame the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Mantega said on Wednesday one option would be to use the fund to soak up extra dollars from a massive share offering planned by \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  that could reach $65 billion.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the government introduced a tax on capital inflows to curb a 34 percent rally in the real, but the source did not expect this any time soon.\par \par \uc2 "(They) are not thinking of using the IOF at the moment," the source said. "The government is trying to manage expectations to avoid a surprise."\par \par \uc2 The source would not comment on the timing of any possible move, adding that "the sovereign fund will go in without the market knowing."\par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry was more focused on the pace of any move rather than any particular level, the source added.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /|LANGEN|ABN|D|E|RBN|M|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100916e69g001eo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
47
