\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Brazil defends exporters in global  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  battle\b0\par\par\uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 17:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Brazil's finance minister says will fight appreciation\par \par \uc2 * Real slumps 1.1 percent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threats\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  could use sovereign wealth fund to curb rally (Adds details, link to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  graphic, updates price)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Sept 15 (Reuters) - Brazil vowed on Wednesday to defend its exporters as governments worldwide moved to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way of speeding up an economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fighting to curb appreciation of the country's hard-charging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRL=>, now ranked the world's most overvalued major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . A strong real has made exports more expensive and could cause long-term damage to Brazil's industry.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  sought to talk down the real for the second time in two weeks, saying he would not sit on the sidelines "watching the game" while other countries weakened their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the expense of Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to take appropriate measures to stop the real from appreciating," he said at an event in Rio de Janeiro.\par \par \uc2 One option, he said, would be to use Brazil's sovereign wealth fund to soak up extra dollars from a massive share offering planned by state-owned oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 "We're watching every market, every part, for inflows," Mantega said. "If inflows come we'll buy it all. We're not going to leave any excess of dollars in the market."\par \par \uc2 Mantega said other countries are relying on exports to lift their struggling economies.\par \par \uc2 "The United States and Europe are also trying to escape the economic crisis by selling exports. They make their monetary policy weak to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and export more," he added. "We're aware of this and we won't let it happen."\par \par \uc2 Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Wednesday for the first time in six years while Colombia announced fresh measures to weaken its peso and protect its exporters. For details, see [ID:nN15191735] and [ID:nN15145483]\par \par \uc2 FIGHTING A LOSING BATTLE?\par \par \uc2 Despite the Brazilian government's best efforts so far, the real is still trading near its strongest level against the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 Huge foreign investment in Brazil and growing demand for its higher-yielding debt have made the real double in value since President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva took office in 2003. <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Graphic on Brazil's real: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/xuv52p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/xuv52p}}}\uc2 \f2 \par   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. dollar inflows <BRCAPF=ECI> to the country totaled $2.114 billion in the month through Sept. 10, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said on Wednesday. That is almost half the total amount of dollars that has entered Brazil during the whole year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One of the most talked-about measures recently on Sao Paulo's trading floors is the possibility of an auction of reverse swaps, a form of derivative which would have the same effect as buying dollars in the futures market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That speculation also caused the real to weaken 1.1 percent to 1.725 per U.S. dollar <BRBY> on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't know where it came from that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was going to start offering swaps. But just with this, just with the threat, the (real) took a dive," said Joao Medeiros, a partner at Pioneer Corretora, one of Brazil's biggest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  brokerages. "The market is tense."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some analysts, however, believe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is fighting a losing battle. For more see [ID:nN10251620]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank said earlier on Wednesday it had bought a staggering $815 million in dollars on the spot market in the month through Sept. 10. Last Thursday alone, the bank bought more than it did during the whole of February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since last Wednesday the bank has been calling two auctions daily, rather than just one, to buy dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But these auctions are costly, since the bank gets next to nothing in interest for all the dollars that have piled up in its reserves. The same problem would arise if the government used its sovereign wealth fund to buy dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even reverse \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps did not stop a near 70 percent rally in the real when they were last used on a regular basis between 2005 and 2008. (Reporting by Rodrigo Viga Gaier in Rio de Janeiro and Silvio Cascione in Sao Paulo; Writing by Samantha Pearson; Editing by Andrew Hay)\f28 \par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ECONOMY/FXFLOWS (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|Table|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100915e69f0015o\par }\page 