\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Brazil may be outgunned in fight to weaken  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2010\par 00:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Government may be unable to stop appreciation of real\par \par \uc2 * Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts carry heavy fiscal cost\par \par \uc2 * Markets betting that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain overvalued (Repeats Friday story for additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Brazil is locked in a bitter struggle with investors over the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- a battle it is unlikely to win without major casualties.\par \par \uc2 The government is increasingly desperate to halt the appreciation of the real <BRL=>, which has more than doubled in value against the dollar since President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva took office in 2003 and is now, by one measure, the world's most overvalued major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise is in part a reflection of Lula's success in transforming Brazil into a darling of foreign investors. Yet what was once seen as a blessing has become a curse, and Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  warned Thursday that the government will take whatever measures are necessary to keep the real from further damaging exporters. [ID:nN09214757]\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has resorted to unusual, multiple \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market this week to try to keep the real at or above its current level of 1.72 per dollar, near its strongest level this year. <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Graphic on Brazil's real: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/xuv52p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/xuv52p }}}\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 Yet Brazil's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  may very well be outgunned in the near-term by a host of economic fundamentals and one-off events, namely the $65 billion share offering planned by state oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  later this month. Analysts say that could unleash further deals and yet another wave of foreign investment inflows.\par \par \uc2 Continuing to fight the real's appreciation also carries potential fiscal costs that may prove too much to bear at a time when the government is already ramping up spending ahead of presidential elections on Oct. 3.\par \par \uc2 In fact, markets are increasingly betting that the government will blink. Wagers by foreign investors on a stronger real reached $14.1 billion as of Sep. 3, according to Brazil's Banco Safra -- not far below an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 "While most central banks carry a certain clout, markets have a tendency to become a groundswell of opinion backed by real positions, with real risk on the line," says Andrew Wilkinson at Interactive Brokers.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL: A VICTIM OF ITS OWN SUCCESS\par \par \uc2 Brazil's combination of robust 7 percent annual economic growth and some of the world's highest interest rates -- the benchmark rate is at 10.75 percent -- has made it an irresistible target for foreign investors at a time when most of the world is dominated by low yields and weak growth.\par \par \uc2 The global growth hunt has also pushed Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <PEN=PE> close to an all-time high and boosted the Chilean peso <CLP=> to its strongest level in eight months.\par \par \uc2 But the real is the most overvalued of all the world's major 33 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Against the U.S. dollar, the real is overvalued by a whopping 53 percent, it says.\par \par \uc2 Even the threat of a double-dip recession in the United States seems to have had little effect on the real in recent weeks, says Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon in New York.\par \par \uc2 "The Brazilian real is hanging in there -- it's very tough, very strong. It seems to be trading off its own fundamentals rather than international investor risk appetite or the performance of the U.S. economy," he says.\par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  insists that it is not trying to fix Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a particular level, economists point to 1.70 real-per-dollar as the government's breaking point.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doubled its dollar purchases on the spot market last month, buying $3.04 billion, its biggest monthly purchase so far this year. On Wednesday, the bank took the unusual step of calling two, rather than one, auctions -- the first time that had happened since May.\par \par \uc2 The bank did the same thing on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Yet, in a potentially foreboding sign, investors have barely flinched. The real closed stronger on the local spot market on both Wednesday and Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  also carry a heavy cost, adding to \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growing debt burden. Most of the dollars the bank buys are held in reserve in U.S. Treasuries, which are currently yielding next to nothing. Meanwhile, the bank still has to pay out much higher interest rates in reais.\par \par \uc2 MIXED RECORD OF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have to look too far into its own past to see that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  often don't work out.\par \par \uc2 Lula's predecessor as president, Fernando Henrique Cardoso, spent billions of dollars trying to maintain the real's value when it was loosely pegged to the dollar in the late 1990s. The peg ultimately buckled, causing a traumatic devaluation.\par \par \uc2 Other countries such as China have got \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to a fine art, keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exactly where they want it.\par \par \uc2 If the real continues to appreciate, Brazilian authorities could resort to weapons of mass destruction such as raising a 2 percent tax on foreign purchases of bonds and stocks that was imposed last October during another bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.\par \par \uc2 That would help to reduce foreign capital inflows. But it could also scare away the same investment that is helping to plug the country's gaping current account deficit, which reached a monthly record in July and is a source of growing concern. [ID:nN23194162]\par \par \uc2 Another option would be for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to employ so-called "reverse swaps", a form of derivative that would have the same effect as buying dollars in the futures market. However, a previous period of swaps between 2005 and 2008 failed to stem a significant appreciation of the real.\par \par \uc2 The mere threat of such measures has been enough to make some investors wary.\par \par \uc2 PETROBRAS IMPACT\par \par \uc2 Diego Donadio, an analyst at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Sao Paulo, says the possibility of greater \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the main reason that he is now advising his clients to buy other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of the real, such as the Mexican peso.\par \par \uc2 "There are so many deals in the pipeline, whether they be bonds or acquisitions and the capitalization of \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 . But on the other hand the risk from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very high," he says.\par \par \uc2 Although many questions remain unanswered about the mechanics of the \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  capitalization, traders expect the deal to lure between $15 billion and $25 billion of foreign investment to Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Since other local companies have been afraid to come to market in recent months, for fear of being upstaged by the oil giant, a deluge of smaller deals could also follow.\par \par \uc2 Flavia Cattan-Naslausky, a strategist at RBS Securities is among those who believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may choose to surrender somewhat to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally during this period.\par \par \uc2 Recent comments from Mantega show "some resignation to short-term appreciation around these large corporate deals," she says.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Silvio Cascione in Sao Paulo. Editing by Brian Winter and W Simon )\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-REAL (ANALYSIS) (REPEAT)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|ABX|BNX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100912e69c000f8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
