\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  rally challenges cenbanks\b0\par\par\uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 19:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso jumps 1 pct after strong U.S. data\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real pares gains ahead of public holidays\par \par \uc2 * Yields on Brazil rates rise after national GDP data (Adds comments, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson and Jean Luis Arce\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/MEXICO CITY, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Brazil's and Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit multi-month highs on Friday, putting pressure on the countries' central banks to take more aggressive action to protect exporters.\par \par \uc2 The recent rallies could reduce demand for exports as strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  make them more expensive abroad, potentially weakening the countries' current accounts -- the broadest measure of trade in goods and services.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  got a boost after U.S. monthly employment data came in much stronger than expected, raising investors' confidence in the global economic recovery. [ID:nN02227856]\par \par \uc2 But caution ahead of a public holiday in the United States on Monday and in Brazil on Tuesday caused the Brazilian real to pare gains in late trading.\par \par \uc2 The real <BRBY> hit its strongest level since Dec. 4, 2009 against the U.S. dollar in early trading. But it eventually closed only 0.1 percent stronger at 1.729 reais on the local spot market.\par \par \uc2 It still recorded a weekly gain of 1.27 pct -- its biggest weekly rise in 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Separate data showing Brazil's economy grew more than expected in the second quarter also boosted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nN03239813]\par \par \uc2 The real is likely to trade around the 1.73-1.74 range next week against the dollar if there are no big changes to the outlook for global growth, said Raphael Martello, an analyst with Tendencias consultancy in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 "It depends a lot on the external scenario. Because the agenda is pretty light in the United States next week compared to this week, it should remain around the current level."\par \par \uc2 If the real breaks below 1.70, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively in the market, he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  said on Friday the government was determined not to allow the real to appreciate as a fund-raising plan by state oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 , the world's largest offering, moves forward.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  deal is expected to lure huge amounts of investment to the country. [ID:nN02239445]\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso <CLP=> hit its strongest level in more than seven months, later paring gains to trade 0.30 percent stronger at 495.70 per dollar. Over the week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was up 0.77 percent.\par \par \uc2 Analysts see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the peso's rise if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firms to a range of 480 to 465 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <MXN=> strengthened 1 percent to 12.9415 per dollar after the U.S. data -- its highest in more than a week. But over the week, it has gained only 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Mexico has gradually limped out of a deep recession thanks to demand for its exports from the United States.\par \par \uc2 "The fear that the United States will fall back into recession is fading every day and because of this we could see a good rally in the peso next week," said Ramses Villela, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Bulltick Capital Markets in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 YIELDS RISE AFTER GDP DATA\par \par \uc2 Yields rose on Brazilian interest rate futures <0#DIJ:> as investors bet Brazil's strong growth data would put more pressure on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to combat inflation by raising the cost of borrowing.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the contract due January 2012 <DIJF2>, one of the most heavily traded contracts of the early session, rose to 11.37 percent from 11.31 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Everything suggests that there is very strong domestic demand here, which could push interest rates higher," said Gradual Investimentos' Perfeito.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday held its benchmark interest rate at 10.75 percent, ending a monetary tightening cycle that helped cool a red-hot economy as the country gears up for a presidential election in October.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  missed a golden opportunity at its last meeting (by not raising rates) to make a smooth transition between this and the next administration. I think this is where they went wrong," said Perfeito. (Additional reporting by Maria Jose Latorre in Santiago; Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100906e6930013y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
