\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Peru's  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  at 2-yr high, region struggles\b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2010\par 16:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real loses 0.5 pct, Mexican peso dips 0.1 pct\par \par \uc2 * World Cup fever partly to blame for Brazil June slowdown\par \par \uc2 * Chilean peso firms as investors price in rate hike\par \par \uc2 (Recasts; adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson and Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/MEXICO CITY, Aug 3 (Reuters) - Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed to its strongest level in two years on Tuesday, after a Brazilian industrial conglomerate became the latest company to take its share of one of the world's fastest growing economies.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Votorantim announced that it had finally gained control of Peruvian miner Milpo on Tuesday. The Peruvian sol <PEN=PE> firmed 0.4 pct to 2.8050 as the Brazilian company moved to snap up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to complete the purchase of its latest stake, said traders in Peru's capital Lima.\par \par \uc2 The deal helped Peruvian markets shrug off a further battering of weak U.S. data which prompted losses among the continent's major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has surged about 16 percent since its low in March 2009 as the country's economy has bounced back from the financial crisis thanks to a huge construction boom.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth is expected to hit 6.6 percent in 2010, the government said in June, ranking Peru alongside Brazil as the fastest growing countries in the region and the world.\par \par \uc2 "Market players are expecting a greater appreciation of the nuevo sol, due to the strong fundamentals of Peru's economy and more dollars coming in thanks to higher exports and private investment," said Jose Razuri, analyst at the Maximixe consultancy in Lima.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=CL> also firmed on Tuesday, gaining 0.3 percent to 516.50 per dollar to hit a fresh three-month intraday high against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 After a devastating earthquake in February, Chile's economy has sprung back faster than most economists were expecting. And strong growth has prompted investors to bet the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to raise interest rates by more than expected over the coming months in an effort to contain inflation.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL AND MEXICO LOSE OUT\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil and Mexico, the dual powerhouse of Latin American growth, suffered losses on Tuesday due to concerns that the U.S. recovery is faltering.\par \par \uc2 Data showed U.S. factory orders fell steeply in June while an index of pending home sales plunged to a record low in the same month.\par \par \uc2 U.S. growth is vital for Latin America's own economic health since the region exports huge amounts of agricultural and mineral goods to the United States.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <MXN=> dipped 0.1 percent to 12.5830 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real was under further pressure from a combination of local concerns, weakening 0.5 percent to 1.758.\par \par \uc2 A drop in industrial production in Brazil during June added to recent signs that growth in Latin America's biggest economy is slowing. For details, see [ID:nN03274323]\par \par \uc2 "The weak industrial performance in the second quarter confirms the expectation of a sharp slowdown of the economy during that period," said Luciano Rostagno, the chief strategist for CM Capital Markets in Sao Paulo, in a note to clients. However, the recent World Cup soccer tournament may have affected production, since many fans in Brazil took days off work to watch the games, he said.\par \par \uc2 Investors were also cautious after the Brazilian real broke through the key 1.75 level in the previous session, prompting further speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Ursula Scollo in Lima and Alonso Soto in Santiago; Editing by Diane Craft)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|D|E|RBN|M|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100806e683001ru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
