\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Closes Higher On Euro, Dollar-Inflow Bets\b0\par\par\uc2 419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2010\par 22:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real closed stronger against the U.S. dollar Thursday, tracking the euro and global commodities prices and ahead of dollar inflows expected in the near term.\par \par \uc2 The real closed at BRL1.7610 to the dollar, stronger than Wednesday's close at BRL1.7650.\par \par \uc2 Global investors recovered some risk appetite on news of a bigger-than-expected increase in an index of economic sentiment in the EU in July, strengthening the value of Europe's common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and weakening the dollar against global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the real.\par \par \uc2 The real also benefited from rising oil and metals prices, which are a boon for Brazil's export revenue.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the real continues to gain on expectations of a big influx of dollars in the short term due to equity and debt offerings by Brazilian companies. Counting on the inflows and betting on a weakening dollar, local banks have accumulated sizable short dollar positions.\par \par \uc2 That situation led \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to indicate it may resume \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures market to support the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Traders also indicated the market was beginning to experience greater volatility in the run-up to Friday's close-out of monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures contracts. The contracts will be settled based on Friday's closing exchange rate, known as the Ptax rate.\par \par \uc2 In the interest rate futures market, all eyes were on the minutes from the meeting of Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. The minutes were published Thursday. At last week's meeting, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted its Selic base rate by 50 basis points to 10.75%.\par \par \uc2 The increase was less than the 75-basis-point increase many analysts had expected. The minutes confirmed that the monetary authoriites are seeing a benign inflation scenario in the near term and are likely to scale back the pace of monetary tightening.\par \par \uc2 Short-term interest rate futures contracts were lower and longer-term contracts higher Thursday, reflecting market adjustment to indications of a slower monetary tightening in the near term followed by renewed inflationary pressures in 2012 or 2013.\par \par \uc2 The most actively traded interest rate futures contract on the Brazilian Financial Exchange, that of January 2012, fell to 11.53% after closing Monday at 11.56%. But the April, 2013 contract closed at 11.97%, up from 11.95% Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Todd Martinez, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7082; brazil@dowjones.com [ 29-07-10 2032GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100729e67t001ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
