\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil's real gives up gains after inflow boost\b0\par\par\uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2010\par 17:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Unexpected drop in U.S. durable goods hits region\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real under pressure over reverse swaps\par \par \uc2 * Chile's peso loses 0.7 pct on industrial slowdown (Adds details about Brazil inflows, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson and Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/MEXICO CITY, July 28 (Reuters) - Brazil's real outperformed Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for most of the session on Wednesday on data showing strong inflows, a day after the government sold bonds at a record-low yield.\par \par \uc2 But the effect was short-lived and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened in late trading, following the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  downward, under pressure from weaker-than-expected U.S. data.\par \par \uc2 Brazil on Tuesday priced $750 million of its reopened 2021 global bond at a 4.547 percent yield, and it sold $75 million more in Asian markets on Wednesday, the Treasury said.\par \par \uc2 The bond sale is set to encourage local banks and companies to also raise money in the debt markets, bringing more dollars to Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Strong inflows related to existing deals in the market helped boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  temporarily on Wednesday, traders in Sao Paulo said. About $1.2 billion entered the market at the beginning of the session due to the actions of two large exporters, they said.\par \par \uc2 "Everything is indicating there was a big inflow today," said Joao Medeiros, a partner at Pioneer Corretora in Sao Paulo. "Volume spiked a lot."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volumes on the Brazilian exchange surged to more than $4 billion on Wednesday, above this year's daily average of $2.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 Brazil offers some of the highest yields among emerging market assets in its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, which are a magnet for investors seeking high short-term returns.\par \par \uc2 But the real <BRBY> closed 0.1 percent weaker at 1.769 per dollar on the local spot market, under pressure from worries about a slowdown in the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 Recent chatter regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the futures market via derivatives by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also "increasing the likelihood of a sharp downward correction looking forward," Diego Donadio, an analyst at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , wrote in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's finance minister, Guido Mantega, on Tuesday also voiced his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. For details, see [ID:nN2798713]\par \par \uc2 The real's sharp gains since the beginning of last year have put huge pressure on exporters, prompting the government last October to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise by imposing a tax on foreign purchases of stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been holding daily dollar auctions to capture excess greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian Treasury Secretary Arno Augustin said on Wednesday Brazil will return to international debt markets before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 The issue would probably be for longer-term paper than the 2021 global bond that was reopened on Tuesday, he said, adding that he would not rule out a foreign issue denominated in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 MEXICO, CHILE \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  SLIP\par \par \uc2 Mexico and Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped on Wednesday after U.S. data showing an unexpected drop in new orders for long-lasting manufactured goods in June renewed concerns the U.S. economic recovery is slowing down.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=> <MEX01> lost 0.4 percent to 12.720. Mexico, Latin America's second-biggest economy, depends on sustained demand in the United States, which buys around 80 percent of local exports.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso was under further pressure after data showed industrial output was slowing in Chile. The country's peso <CLP=CL> bid 0.7 percent weaker at 523.30 per dollar in afternoon trading.\par \par \uc2 Chile's industrial production <CLIP=ECI> rose a seasonally adjusted 0.8 percent in June from May, falling short of market expectations of a 2.25 percent increase.\par \par \uc2 "The slower growth in industrial output is prompting the expectation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could decide to put the brakes on its benchmark rate increases, which the peso is now reflecting," one trader in Santiago said.\par \par \uc2 The market has been betting on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raising its key rate by another 50 basis points to 2 percent at its next monetary policy meeting. A bank poll of financial players released on Wednesday showed that economists expect the rate to be at around 3.63 percent within six months.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100731e67s000a3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
