\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil Finance Min Sees $45-$48B Current Account Deficit\b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2010\par 18:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 27 Jul 2010 12:58 EDT *DJ  Brazil Finance Minister: Imports Impacting Deficit\par \par \uc2 27 Jul 2010 13:00 EDT *DJ  Brazil Finance Min: Current Account Deficit Not A Threat\par \par \uc2 27 Jul 2010 13:02 EDT *DJ  Brazil Finance Min Favors \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Action On \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 27 Jul 2010 13:17 EDT =DJ  Brazil Finance Minister Sees $45B-$48B Current Account Deficit\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil faces a sharp rise in its current account deficit in 2010, but the increase doesn't spell a threat to Brazil's overseas accounts or its booming economy, Finance Minister Guido Mantega said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Mantega predicted a current account deficit in 2010 that will be between $45 billion and $48 billion. The current account deficit in 2009 was $24.3 billion.\par \par \uc2 Mantega made the comments to reporters and was quoted by the local Estado news agency.\par \par \uc2 "The deficit does not represent any kind of threat," said Mantega. "It does not threaten economic growth. On the contrary, it is the result of economic growth."\par \par \uc2 Mantega said growth in the deficit was coming mainly from an increase in imports due to Brazil's booming economy. The economy is likely to expand some 7% this year, according to most economists.\par \par \uc2 Burgeoning imports have pulled down Brazil's trade surplus so far this year. Through July 25, Brazil posted a trade surplus of $9.36 billion, down from $16.71 billion for the same period in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Mantega also commented on Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, saying he favors \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "monitoring" of the market to guard against excessive volatility.\par \par \uc2 He added, "I favor \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, when opportune." He said such action could include \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in past years, has conducted swap auctions involving \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate futures contracts as a way to reduce volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also acts in the market as a buyer and seller of U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7090; brazil@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 27, 2010 13:17 ET (17:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100727e67r004k8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
