\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  slip on US data, Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2010\par 16:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real loses 0.8 pct ahead of China data\par \par \uc2 * Gloomier Fed outlook dents sentiment\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso hurt by drop in U.S. retail sales\par \par \uc2 * Chile's peso firms on rate hike bets\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Samantha Pearson\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY/SAO PAULO, July 14 (Reuters) - Brazil and Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dipped on Wednesday as weak U.S. retail sales and concerns about upcoming Chinese economic growth data threatened to derail a recent rally on bets of solid U.S. corporate earnings.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real slipped after hitting a two-month high on Tuesday, while the Mexican peso retreated from its strongest in two weeks. Chile's peso firmed ahead of a widely expected interest rate hike on Thursday by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also weighing on sentiment was a gloomier outlook on the U.S. economy from the Federal Reserve. Members of its interest-rate setting board said they had "slightly" revised down their outlook for economic growth. [ID:nWALEIE6D2]\par \par \uc2 "If the U.S. economy is not going to grow as much as expected, then Mexico as well will not grow as much, and that will hurt exports and the peso," said Alejandro Martinez, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 U.S. data showed retail sales fell in June for the second straight month, which offset better-than-expected results from technology bellwether \uc2 Intel Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There is a tug of war. People are worried about growth in the second half, but then you have these stellar earnings," said Win Thin, an emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Riskier assets like emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  got a boost this month on bets that U.S. corporate earnings could compensate for a string of recent weak data in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Trading on Latin American stock exchanges was choppy on Wednesday. [ID:nN14143185]\par \par \uc2 Investors cut risky positions ahead of economic growth data due early Thursday from China, Brazil's top trading partner. Economists expect China's year-on-year growth rate slowed in the second quarter. [ID:nSGE6660AR]\par \par \uc2 "China could either throw gasoline on the fire and people will start worrying about the global slowdown again. But I think that the Chinese economy remains pretty strong, so hopefully it will underpin sentiment," Thin said.\par \par \uc2 CHILE RATE BETS\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY> bid 0.8 percent weaker at 1.765 reais per U.S. dollar on the local spot market.\par \par \uc2 Analysts also pointed to concerns of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could have pushed some to pare back on bets backing the real. Traders say policy makers are uncomfortable seeing the real stronger than 1.75 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=> lost 0.31 percent to 12.7224 after hitting its strongest since June 28 early in the session.\par \par \uc2 The slack U.S. retail sales data for June put pressure on the peso.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is closely tied to the health of the U.S. economy given that the United States buys about 80 percent of Mexico's exports.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso <CLP=> firmed 0.5 percent to 532.35 per dollar, helped by speculation that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise interest rates more than a widely-expected 50 basis-point hike to 1.5 percent on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Both Brazil and Chile are raising interest rates, which should keep up demand from yield-hungry investors for local debt.\par \par \uc2 Chile's economy has made a quick recovery from the effects of a massive earthquake in February, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more reason to tighten monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso is seen firming to 521.63 per U.S. dollar by the end of the year, as rising rates attract greater foreign investment, a poll by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed on Wednesday. [ID:nN14122954] (Additional reporting by Brad Haynes in Santiago; Editing by Padraic Cassidy)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100717e67e0009j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
