\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Opens Stronger, Shrugs Off  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Threats\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeff Fick   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 530 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2010\par 15:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real opened stronger against the U.S. dollar Friday on expectations this week's interest rate hike will boost investment inflows, as the market shrugged off threats that the government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The real opened at BRL1.7260 to the dollar on the BM&FBovespa exchange, stronger from Thursday's close at BRL1.7302.\par \par \uc2 Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets dismissed threats made by Treasury Secretary Arno Augustin on Thursday that the treasury was prepared to start buying dollars to counter any steep appreciation in the real.\par \par \uc2 The problem, traders said, is that until the treasury actually starts buying dollars, nobody believes the threat is credible.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, has been slightly more aggressive in recent weeks. On Thursday, the bank purchased dollars from the spot market at two separate snap auctions--the second time in April the bank has held two dollar purchase auctions.\par \par \uc2 The bank has held a single daily purchase auction since May 2009. Before this month, the last double auction was held in 2007.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have been flooding into Brazil in recent months as the country's strong recovery from last year's brief recession reinforced prospects for growth. Furthermore, Brazil's interest rates remain among the highest in the world.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the benchmark Selic base interest rate by 75 basis points. The Selic now stands at 9.5%.\par \par \uc2 The bank is also expected to front-load a series of rate hikes aimed at controlling inflationary pressures and avoiding an overheated economy, according to economists.\par \par \uc2 Higher interest rates in Brazil typically attract heavy foreign inflows from investors seeking better rates of return. Brazil has been a favored destination for carry trade players, who borrow cheap cash overseas and invest in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Also fueling the real during Friday's session was the typical month-end battle to set the Ptax, which is the daily average \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate used to settle futures contracts. The monthly tug-of-war between long- and short-position holders usually adds a dose of volatility to trading.\par \par \uc2 Market players currently shorting the dollar have pushed hard to devalue the greenback in recent sessions, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The market, however, could enter into defensive positions as concerns about debt-troubled European Union countries remain, traders said. A resolution to the debt crisis in Greece could come as early as the weekend.\par \par \uc2 European Union officials have been adamant that they will not allow Greece to become the first euro-zone country to default on its debt. Greece is currently in talks with the EU and International Monetary Fund about a bailout package to aid the debt-laden nation.\par \par \uc2 Recent downgrades to the sovereign debt of Portugal and Spain had also turned markets jittery amid fears Greece's issues would spread elsewhere in the EU.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Brazil's benchmark Global 40 bond was 3/8 higher at a bid of 134 5/16.\par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085; Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 30-04-10 1342GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0