\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil Official: Govt Mulling Whether To Levy IOF Tax - Estado\b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2009\par 23:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)--Brazilian government advisers are discussing whether the timing is right to levy the country's IOF financial transactions tax on incoming foreign investment as a way to curb the inflow of dollars and appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Foreign Commerce Secretary Welber Barral said Monday, according to the Estado news agency.\par \par \uc2 "The discussion within the government is if it's the right moment, or not, or whether the time has already passed," said Barral.\par \par \uc2 He noted that the recent appreciation of Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, has begun to hurt the country's exports. The real has strengthened more than 15% against the dollar since the beginning of the year.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Trade Ministry Monday reported that the country's foreign trade surplus narrowed in May to $2.65 billion from $3.71 billion in April. According to the latest weekly market survey released by Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the trade surplus is expected to decline to $20 billion this year from $24.7 billion in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Barral said the government would prefer to see foreign investment inflows limited by falling local interest rates rather than tax increases or \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut the country's reference Selic interest rate by 3.5 percentage points so far this year to 10.25% annually. The bank is seen cutting the rate to as low as 9% through the end of this year, according to market estimates.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega and \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles last week said that although the government keeping an increase in the IOF tax among its arsenal of possible measures to reduce foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows, it had no immediate plans to raise the tax.\par \par \uc2 -By Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; (55-61) 3335-0832; gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 01, 2009 17:26 ET (21:26 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090826e5610lv5i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
