\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Central Bk Acts On Real Rally But Draws No Line In Sand\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alastair Stewart   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2009\par 17:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the upward march of the Brazilian real over the last week, but it was unwilling to draw a line in the sand.\par \par \uc2 Last Tuesday, Brazil offered to swap $3.4 billion in long positions in the Brazilian real for interest rate-linked paper to take pressure off the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the futures market. It then repeated the dose in spot trading, buying dollars on Friday and Monday. But the volumes are seen as too timid to reverse the real's rally.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not buying dollars in quantities that would revert the trend. It doesn't want to set bands," said Nick Chamie, head of emerging-markets research at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, investors have shed concerns that the global recession will deepen and flocked back to the emerging-market destinations abandoned during the financial meltdown in the third quarter of last year.\par \par \uc2 Those investment flows have been backed up by dollars from exports, which have proven more resilient to the global downturn than many expected.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the real has gone on a massive tear, rising 14% against the dollar since the start of April to trade at about BRL2.05 as investors unwind safe-haven dollar positions and Brazil regains its status as the darling of the equities investment world.\par \par \uc2 Identifying a strong tail wind, local banks piled into long positions in reals on the futures market, accentuating the gains.\par \par \uc2 That was the trigger for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.\par \par \uc2 Officially, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it is buying dollars to replenish reserves, although few believe that.\par \par \uc2 However, its tentative dollar purchases on the spot market indicate that it is offering a reference, rather than trying to set a band, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "It is providing a signal that it isn't comfortable with the dollar below BRL2.00 at a time when exports are just starting to recover. It wouldn't be foolish enough to try and defend that, though," said Joao Medeiros, partner at Pioneer brokerage, Brazil's biggest interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broker.\par \par \uc2 In the first dollar auction in eight months Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought between $250 million and $300 million.\par \par \uc2 In similar auctions held on a near-daily basis on occasions between October 2006 and September 2008, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought an average of around $500 million, but market volume were much greater then.\par \par \uc2 According to Ilan Goldfajn, chief economist at Itau Unibanco, Brazil's largest bank, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can easily afford to accumulate another $5 billion to $10 billion in reserves. But any more than that would start to create inflationary pressure, said the former \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  director.\par \par \uc2 If the current appetite for Brazilian assets persists, the real could march well past BRL2.00 per dollar, although it probably wouldn't reach the eight-year high of BRL1.56 to the dollar registered in August of 2008.\par \par \uc2 However, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles last week went as far as to warn investors about excessive optimism about Brazil's prospects for recovery and counseled them that they could be setting themselves up for disappointment.\par \par \uc2 -By Alastair Stewart, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-2847-4520; alastair.stewart@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Tom Murphy in Sao Paulo contributed to this report.) [ 05-12-09 1130ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020090512e55c000h8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
