\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Fiscal discipline to help Brazil through crisis-govt\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ana Nicolaci da Costa and Isabel Versiani   \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 16:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, March 5 (Reuters) - Brazil will brave the financial crisis by sticking to austere fiscal policies, a strategy that should help it grow above the global average, the country's top two economic officials said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Slowing growth and falling tax revenues fanned fears Brazil would abandon the kind of fiscal discipline that helped the country revamp its public accounts, earned it an investment grade and made it a favorite among investors in recent years.\par \par \uc2 While many countries around the world are spending billions of dollars to counteract a global recession, Brazil's government has sought to strike a delicate balance between targeted spending on investments and fiscal restraint so as to keep its debt burden manageable.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue with a serious fiscal policy, balanced, so that we will have to cut current expenditures to be able to adjust to the drop in tax receipts that is actually taking place because of the crisis," Finance Minister Guido Mantega said in a speech at an economic conference in Brasilia.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Mantega said the government would decide by March 20 if it will lower its primary budget surplus target this year from 3.8 percent of gross domestic product currently to free up cash for stimulus spending.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's 12-month primary budget surplus -- which excludes interest payments and is closely watched by investors as a gauge of a country's ability to service its debt -- already fell below that target in January to 3.58 percent, its lowest level in five years.\par \par \uc2 But officials reinforced they would stick to planned investments, especially in the Program to Accelerate Growth, which aims to improve the country's aging infrastructure, including roads, railways and houses.\par \par \uc2 "We will maintain all of the investment programs," Mantega added.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL GROWTH\par \par \uc2 Balanced fiscal policies and anti-crisis measures of the past months would put Brazil in a good place to brave the global financial crisis, officials said.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil will grow less this year than in previous ones, it's a very difficult situation but will grow more than the global average, will be among the first countries to come out of the crisis and will be stronger after the crisis," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles said at the same event.\par \par \uc2 The government has taken a series of measures ranging from selling dollars on the spot market to meet growing demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to giving tax breaks to hard-hit industries to try to alleviate the impact of the crisis on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Meirelles said \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent $14.5 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since early October, some $9.7 billion in dollar repurchase agreements and another $9.6 billion in foreign trade lines through March 2.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government is also considering extending the temporary reduction of taxes on industrial goods that have helped sales in the automobile sector for a longer period of time, Trade and Industry Minister Miguel Jorge told reporters on the sidelines of the same event.\par \par \uc2 Despite all the efforts, analysts expect economic growth to slow to 1.5 percent in 2009 from above 5 percent growth last year, with some economists already forecasting zero growth or a contraction.\par \par \uc2 Mantega also acknowledged that credit problems continue as it remains scarce and costly despite government efforts to facilitate lending.\par \par \uc2 "There is a lack of credit in the economy," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Kenneth Barry)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ECONOMY/MANTEGA (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090305e535001bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
