\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Opens Weaker, Tracks Other Risky  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkts\b0\par\par\uc2 471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2009\par 13:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's real opened weaker Friday, tracking many other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as risk averse investors go long on the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The real opened at BRL2.391 per dollar after closing Thursday at BRL2.351 on the Brazilian Mercantile and Futures Exchange, or the BM&F.\par \par \uc2 The real is facing the brunt of poor investor sentiment towards many emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week and Friday's start was no exception to the trend.\par \par \uc2 The real has been strengthening this month due to a billion dollar bond offering by oil company \uc2 Petroleo Brasileiro\uc2  (PBR), but that money has already entered the market.\par \par \uc2 "International investors are long U.S. dollar on the BM&F," said Plinio Camargo, a dollar futures broker at Flow Corretora in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 "They are seeing a deteriorating scenario around the world and betting against the real," he said.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors are holding around $11.5 billion in dollar hedges on the BM&F. Some of those holdings are hedges against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange risks for funds that have holdings in Brazilian bonds and equities. Funds also use the BM&F to lock in a dollar rate so when they sell Brazilian stocks and bonds they do not lose value on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when those positions are cashed in for dollars.\par \par \uc2 Local brokers are divided over whether or not the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market Friday. If the dollar breaks BRL2.40, some say the government could come in as a dollar seller.\par \par \uc2 One brokerage said that he was bringing in around $200 million into Brazil through the Inter-American Development Bank later in the day.\par \par \uc2 "Mark my words, the government won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  today," the broker said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  also starts its popular Carnaval holiday Friday. Markets will be closed on Monday and Tuesday and open half day on Wednesday, "so many companies are not willing to play around too much with their dollar positions today," said Francisco Giminez, a broker at NGO Corretora do Cambio in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Giminez said he suspects a volatile day, nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 Other major emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also trading weaker against the U.S. dollar on Friday, including the Indian Rupee, the Singapore and Taiwan dollars.\par \par \uc2 The euro has declined sharply against the dollar this week and is down against the Japanese yen Friday, as investors are worried about euro-zone banks active in Eastern Europe.\par \par \uc2 The distaste for emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was felt in Brazilian trade early in the day on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Camargo expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the market gets too shaky and the dollar rises above BRL2.40.\par \par \uc2 -By Kenneth Rapoza, Dow Jones Newswires; (5511) 2847-4541,kenneth.rapoza@dowjones.com [ 20-02-09 1305GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090220e52k000kl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
